106: - EBITDA margins trending toward mid-single digit in 2027 from high single digit in 2024\nL107: - Capex in line with investment guarantee scheme\nL108: - 2024 net working capital change driven by inventory during model switch\nL109: - Successful placement of Eurobond\nL110: - Dividend pay-out ratio at 50%\nL111: RATING SENSITIVITIES\nL112: Factors that Could, Individually or Collectively, Lead to Positive Rating Action/Upgrade:\nL113: - An upgrade of FMC or strengthening of legal incentives for FMC to support Ford Otosan\nL114: - Net cash position\nL115: - Through-the-cycle FCF margin sustained above 4%\nL116: Factors that Could, Individually or Collectively, Lead to Negative Rating Action/Downgrade:\nL117: - EBITDA margin sustainably below 6%\nL118: - Through-the-cycle FCF margin sustained below 1%\nL119: https://www.fitchratings.com/research/corporate-finance/fitch-publishes-ford-otosan-bb-idr-outlook-stable-rates-proposed-eurobond-bb-exp-11-04-2024\nL120: 5/13\nL121: 4/11/24, 10:51 AM\nL122: Fitch Publishes Ford Otosan's 'BB+' IDR; Outlook Stable; Rates Proposed Eurobond 'BB+(EXP)'\nL123: - EBITDA net leverage above 2.0x by 2025\nL124: - Multi-notch downgrade of FMC or adverse change to contractual sales to FMC\nL125: LIQUIDITY AND DEBT STRUCTURE\nL126: Comfortable Liquidity: Ford Otosan had TRY5.0 billion of available cash as of end-2023, after Fitch's adjustment for restricted cash.\nL127: Management targets maintaining a level of cash and credit commitments to meet 21 days of working capital outflows. The export\nL128: receivables relate to a single counterparty, which is Ford Europe. The average receivable collection time at Turkish and Romanian plants\nL129: are within 14 days and 30 days, respectively.\nL130: For domestic sales, a direct debit system is used for sales via dealers to mitigate credit risk. Ford Otosan uses letters of credit and trade\nL131: lines for export sales, and has a EUR100 million revolver for working capital needs. We expect negative FCF in the rating horizon\nL132: reflecting a capex spike and ongoing dividend payments.\nL133: Barbell Debt Maturity Profile Expected: Otosan's debt structure comprises both term loan A and term loan Bs. Most term loan Bs and\nL134: the planned Eurobond are expected to have maturity between 2026 and 2028, reducing near-term liquidity needs after the planned\nL135: refinancing. The short-term debt is refinanced on a rolling basis each year.\nL136: ISSUER PROFILE\nL137: Ford Otosan is a Turkish automotive manufacturing company that is specialised in light vehicle production, with leading market shares\nL138: in Europe. The company is a joint venture between Koc Holding (41%) and Ford Motor Company (41%), rest of the shares are free float.\nL139: DATE OF RELEVANT COMMITTEE\nL140: 04 April 2024\nL141: REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResearch Update: (https://www.fordotosan.com.tr/documents/Documents/Diger_Duyurular/sp-rating.pdf)\nciteturn15view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.4 years ago; Content type: application/pdf; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn13search1\",\"pattern\":\"Downside scenario\"}); Redirected to URL: https://www.fordotosan.com.tr/documents/Documents/Diger_Duyurular/sp-rating.pdf#page=1; Total lines: 696\nL180: financial performance, it reserves the right to withdraw or revise the ratings. Potential changes\nL181: include, but are not limited to, usage of the new notes; maturity, size, and conditions of the\nL182: instruments; financial and other covenants; security; and ranking.\nL183: Outlook\nL184: The positive outlook on the preliminary rating on FO mirrors that on Turkiye and our expectation\nL185: that the group will continue to pass our hypothetical sovereign default and T&C stress tests\nL186: sustainably. We also base the outlook on our expectation that FO will maintain adequate liquidity\nL187: and gradually increase its earnings outside its home country.\nL188: Downside scenario\nL189: We could revise our outlook on FO to stable following a similar rating action on Turkiye. We could\nL190: also lower the preliminary rating if the company fails to pass our sovereign default and T&C stress\nL191: tests. Failed tests could arise from setbacks in ramping up production and profitability at FO's\nL192: Romania operations or reduced other sources of hard currency cash to cover foreign debt service\nL193: and hard currency raw material imports.\nL194: Upside scenario\nL195: We could raise our preliminary rating if we take a similar action on Turkiye (including a higher T&C\nL196: assessment), or we estimate that FO can sustainably generate more than 30% of its total earnings\nL197: www.spglobal.com/ratingsdirect\nL198: April 11, 2024       5\nL199: Research Update: Ford Otomotiv Rated Preliminary 'BB-'; Outlook Positive; $500 Million Proposed Unsecured Notes Rated Preliminary 'BB-'\nL200: outside the country while continuing to pass our hypothetical sovereign default and T&C stress\nL201: tests. An upgrade would also hinge on the company maintaining an adequate liquidity position and\nL202: credit metrics in line with our current expectations.\nL203: Company Description\nL204: FO is a licensed manufacturer of light commercial vehicles (Transit, Custom, and Courier models)\n"
